    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d461">

                <pb facs="0452" n="440" sameAs="frus:frus1944v05/pg440"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">891.002/12–544: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Iran</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Morris</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Tehran</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-12-05T13:00:00+03:25">December 5, 1944—1
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 4:55 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>901. Nearly every Deputy who spoke during debate on new Cabinet whether
                    supporting or opposing Bayat took occasion to attack Millspaugh violently.</p>
                <p>At one point in reply to a question the Prime Minister is reported to have said
                    that if one of the world’s greatest political leaders such as Churchill were
                    brought to administer Iran no progress would be made so long as Millspaugh
                    remained.</p>
                <p>In his concluding speech yesterday Bayat said that the question of the foreign
                    employees would be among the first to be studied by the Government and that
                    action would be taken within 10 days to settle the status of the Administrator
                    General of Finances. This promise was enthusiastically received by the
                    Deputies.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Morris</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
